# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine last touched a
# file. This project is written on Windows and runs on Linux, and git's default
# is to convert on checkout — which is harmless for everything except the files
# Linux actually executes or parses.
#
# A shell script with CRLF does not run at all: the kernel reads `#!/usr/bin/env
# bash\r` and reports "bad interpreter", naming a program that plainly exists.
# A systemd unit with CRLF is worse, because it half works. So these are LF,
# always, in the repository and in every checkout of it.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.env.example   text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
